WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
SHORT FORM FATIGUE:
Companies are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:07.980
giving up on short form content. Last month, Meta&nbsp;
announced that they will stop offering bonuses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.920
short form creators. Why you ask? Well, it turns&nbsp;
out that pushing short form content actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.680
loses Meta money. Here’s the thing, while&nbsp;
Instagram reels have exploded in popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:25.500
they’re not exactly stealing marketshare away&nbsp;
from TikTok. Rather, it’s stealing market share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.820
away from themselves. Instead of scrolling&nbsp;
through pictures or the newsfeed on Instagram,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:35.520
people are now scrolling through Reels. This&nbsp;
doesn’t sound like a big deal until you consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.040
that Reels are substantially less profitable&nbsp;
than Instagram’s other products. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:46.800
according to Zuckerberg himself, people switching&nbsp;
to Reels is costing the company $500 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:54.180
every quarter or $2 billion every year. And the&nbsp;
most ironic part is that this entire time Meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:00.420
had been paying creators to make reels. So,&nbsp;
really, they were paying to lose revenue. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.520
wonder they stopped. But, it’s not just Meta&nbsp;
who’s pivoting away from short form content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:11.220
either. TikTok themselves are very much trying&nbsp;
to move away. In fact, they’ve been aggressively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.860
increasing their max video length. What started&nbsp;
at 15 seconds grew to 60 seconds to 3 minutes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:23.700
now 10 minutes. TikTok has even been experimenting&nbsp;
with landscape mode on Android. So, TikTok is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.320
much heading towards the direction of becoming&nbsp;
the YouTube were people don’t search. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.840
it’s not like people search on YouTube all that&nbsp;
much anyway. Most of the time, you just use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.420
home page to find videos. But, what happened?&nbsp;
Why are all these companies pivoting away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.640
shorts? Wasn’t shorts the new medium due to&nbsp;
ever decreasing attention spans and the need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:51.000
for constant stimulation. Well, it turns out&nbsp;
that the answer is no. While short form content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.040
is great at garnering a lot of attention in the&nbsp;
short term, it’s not exactly the best model for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.560
companies or audiences long term. Instagram and&nbsp;
YouTube are starting to realize that they invested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:09.360
way too much money on a low ROI fad, and Tiktok&nbsp;
is fighting hard to not become the next Vine. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.280
here’s why companies got short form content&nbsp;
wrong and why they’re already backing off.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.600
UNLOYAL CREATORS:&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:29.760
All successful creator driven platforms need a&nbsp;
strong base of creators. This is why platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.620
like YouTube and Spotify has even turned&nbsp;
to offering exclusivity deals like its NFL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:39.540
football or something. So, it would be quite&nbsp;
an issue if these platforms were struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:45.060
grow a creator base and that’s very much the case&nbsp;
with short form content. This isn’t to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:50.580
TikTok and Reels aren’t able to garner a bunch of&nbsp;
creators because there’s no shortage of that. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.820
what there is is a shortage of a loyal short form&nbsp;
creators. Here’s the thing, 9 times out of 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.400
people don’t start posting short form content&nbsp;
because that’s what they truly want to do. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.700
creators start posting short form content because&nbsp;
they see opportunity, they see a gold rush. Most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:14.520
creators never find gold but even those who do&nbsp;
don’t want to reinvest the profits into mining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.980
for more, it’s simply too risky. Rather, what they&nbsp;
want to do is sell the gold and start a stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:25.740
business. In other words, they want to switch&nbsp;
to long form content. Much of this attitude can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.600
actually be explained by the algorithms used to&nbsp;
promote short form content. Your follower count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:38.160
really means nothing. This is why its a gold rush.&nbsp;
If you start posting today, you have as good of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.740
a chance of posting a viral video as someone with&nbsp;
10 million followers. Actually, I take that back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.840
if a creator has that many followers, they’re&nbsp;
probably pretty good at making viral content. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.520
the point is that the pivotal factor is&nbsp;
the content itself, not the follower count.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.800
While this is great for new creators, it’s&nbsp;
absolute garbage when it comes to longevity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.900
Creators are forced to always keep up with the&nbsp;
latest trends and put out something that can go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:10.260
viral instead of creating content that’s actually&nbsp;
impactful and meaningful. This is why you see so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.080
many TikTok stars that rise out of nowhere and&nbsp;
then disappear faster than they blew up. Recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.480
I was watching an Airrack video where he trapped&nbsp;
25 Tiktokers inside his house. Many of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:27.600
creators had millions if not tens of millions of&nbsp;
followers. So, I don’t know if it’s just me but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.240
I had no idea who any of these people were. Let&nbsp;
me know down below if you were in the same boat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:38.880
but anway, even for creators who enjoy making&nbsp;
short form content want to switch to long form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.240
because it’s simply the smarter business decision.&nbsp;
That covers basically 90% of short form creators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:52.560
The other 10% are actually long form creators.&nbsp;
Personally, I’m guilty of this as well. A lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.760
creators have figured that they might as well&nbsp;
try adapting their regular long form content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:03.900
to short form. The mentality is usually, if it&nbsp;
works, great, if it doesn’t, not a big deal. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.540
little to say, no creator on TikTok, Instagram&nbsp;
Reels, or YouTube Shorts is really loyal to said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:15.000
platform. For all of them, short form content&nbsp;
is really just a springboard to garner attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:20.640
or a medium to expand their existing long form&nbsp;
content. So, when you’re trying to run a creator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.700
driven platform with such people, well let’s&nbsp;
just say it’s difficult to say the least. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:30.720
building up a loyal creator base is just&nbsp;
the first of these platform’s worries.

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:39.720
HESITANT ADVERTISERS:&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:44.640
Not only do these platforms have to deal with&nbsp;
flaky creators, but they also have to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:50.820
with flaky advertisers. The reality is that no&nbsp;
advertiser really wants to advertise on short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:56.700
form content. This isn’t to say that short form&nbsp;
advertising is ineffective. Biaheza, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:01.620
has shown time and time again that short form&nbsp;
advertising is not only effective but it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:08.040
be super lucrative. But, with that being said,&nbsp;
shorts just don’t vibe with most brands. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:13.740
can you imagine GE making a TikTok ad about their&nbsp;
refrigerators or Intel making a Tiktok ad about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:20.460
the new i9 processor. It just doesn't feel right.&nbsp;
Now, some legacy companies are able to pull it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.320
off. A notable example that comes to mind is&nbsp;
Coca Cola. But, just because they’re able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:31.260
make effective ads doesn’t mean that they want to&nbsp;
advertise on these platforms. The reality is that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:37.020
in many cases, these companies just don’t want&nbsp;
to associate with short form content because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:42.120
see it as a risk. I’m sure you’re familiar with&nbsp;
how companies don’t want to associate with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:48.480
following content on YouTube. Well, news flash&nbsp;
this is basically the entirety of TikTok. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.580
let’s even put that aside. Let’s say you’re ok&nbsp;
with advertising next to this type of content.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.620
You still have the challenge of running a&nbsp;
successful ad campaign. While TikTok can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.940
great for promoting products like this, it’s not&nbsp;
exactly great when it comes to building general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:08.520
brand awareness and visibility. Oftentimes, when&nbsp;
companies like Apple or Coca Cola put out an ad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:13.860
they’re not trying to get you buy their product.&nbsp;
They don’t need you to click on the ad or buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:19.980
on the spot. Rather, they just want to build up&nbsp;
their brand presence. This is decently possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:25.980
on YouTube given that viewers are forced to&nbsp;
watch for 5 or 15 seconds. But, on TikTok,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:32.100
if an ad doesn’t pique the viewers interest within&nbsp;
0.2 seconds, they’re gonna swipe up. This means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:37.680
that advertisers are forced to include some sort&nbsp;
of gimmicky hook which again just doesn’t fit with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.500
their brand image. And if you don’t believe me,&nbsp;
you can just look at the numbers. For my usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.860
videos, I generally get an ad rate of $4-$5 per&nbsp;
thousand views. For shorts, I generally get an ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:56.580
rate of 5 cents per thousand views. This basically&nbsp;
indicates that advertisers are 100 times less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:02.160
willing to bid on short form ad slots as opposed&nbsp;
to long form ad slots. It also gives a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.620
context as to why creators want to switch to long&nbsp;
form content so badly. The reality is that if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:13.320
wanna make a living off of short form content, you&nbsp;
have to pull in a braindead amount of views every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:20.160
month. To make just $5000 per month for example,&nbsp;
you have to pull in a 100 million views. Let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.660
phrase that in a different way. You have to&nbsp;
reach 1/8th of the entire world population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:30.660
every single year just to make 60 grand. At&nbsp;
least, creators have the option to do direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:37.260
brand deals or promote their own products. For&nbsp;
these platforms, the only way they make money is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:44.340
through these ads. So, even with their ridiculous&nbsp;
scale, it’s just not all that profitable and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:49.080
no wonder why Meta is losing $2 billion&nbsp;
every year by pushing short form content.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:51.600
A WANING AUDIENCE:&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:09:01.860
While unloyal creators and hesitant&nbsp;
advertisers are no doubt a pain in the neck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.960
atleast there’s some hope with that. As more&nbsp;
modern companies start advertising on short form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.940
platforms, ad rates should go up which should make&nbsp;
creating short form more worthwhile for platforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:18.240
and creators. But, something that these platforms&nbsp;
can’t recover from is a waning audience. The truth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.920
is that people are getting tired of predictable&nbsp;
TikTok content and the main reason for this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:29.040
that these videos rarely have any substance. Every&nbsp;
single video is based around a thirst trap or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.740
sort of gimmick, but rarely do they have any sort&nbsp;
of real takeaway or message. This is why people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:40.080
forget most shorts within 2 minutes of watching&nbsp;
it. It feels good in the moment but the feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.240
doesn't last long. But, wait a minute, isn’t&nbsp;
people’s attention spans getting crushed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:52.500
these platforms? Well, to be honest, I think this&nbsp;
effect is vastly overstated. TikTok is basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.960
like ice cream. People like eating ice cream.&nbsp;
They may even eat it on a daily basis. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:03.480
just because a person eats ice cream doesn’t mean&nbsp;
that they’re suddenly going to stop eating steak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:09.060
or pizza or burgers or real food in general. In&nbsp;
fact, if you had the option of only consuming ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:15.300
cream or pizza, I’m pretty sure that virtually&nbsp;
everyone will choose pizza. Looking back, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.300 --> 00:10:20.640
pandemic forced most people to stay at home which&nbsp;
led to them snacking way more than usual. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.680
as people return to their normal lives, they’re&nbsp;
social media diet is also returning to a real food&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:32.940
and this is already evident in the stats. In 2021,&nbsp;
TikTok rose to be the most popular domain in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:39.960
entire world. But, in 2022, TikTok fell down to&nbsp;
#3 as Google and Facebook regained their leads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:46.080
And looking forward, it seems like this trend will&nbsp;
only continue as TikTok is looking more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:52.740
like Vine. I know that most of you just remember&nbsp;
Vine as a big failure, but Vine was actually super&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:59.520
popular for a period of time. In fact, at the end&nbsp;
of 2015, Vine had as many as 200 million users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:05.340
Why did Vine fail then you ask? Well, you can&nbsp;
take it from the horse’s mouth itself. Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:10.860
the parent company of Vine, was unable and/or&nbsp;
unwilling to pay the creators that made the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:16.500
platform so popular in the first place. This&nbsp;
was actually Jack Dorsey’s biggest regret. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:22.380
what is TikTok doing? Well, they’re unable and/or&nbsp;
unwilling to pay the creators that made their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.780
platform so big. And as audiences and creators&nbsp;
naturally shift back to Facebook and YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:32.820
these platforms see less of a reason to be so&nbsp;
aggressive when it comes to promoting shorts.

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:34.500
THE FATE OF SHORTS:&nbsp;

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:45.660
In the end, it’s not surprising why companies are&nbsp;
backing away from short form content. The reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:51.060
is that creators are just using shorts as a&nbsp;
springboard, advertisers don’t wanna get involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:56.340
and audiences are by no means ditching long&nbsp;
form content. This isn’t to say that TikTok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.340 --> 00:12:01.440
is going to die like Vine, but what has become&nbsp;
abundantly clear is that short form content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:07.260
is simply an addition to long form content not a&nbsp;
replacement for it like the media often suggested.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:13.560
So, Meta and YouTube no longer feel threatened&nbsp;
by TikTok. If anything, it’s actually TikTok that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:19.380
strives to be more like Meta and YouTube so that&nbsp;
they don’t end up like Vine. Combine this with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:25.740
recession, and it simply makes no sense to bet so&nbsp;
much on a historically flaky medium and that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:31.020
companies are already giving up and pulling back&nbsp;
on short form content. Did you ever buy into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:36.660
short form craze? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you’re glad that short form content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:41.220
is dying. And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
discord community to suggest future video ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:44.580
and consider subscribing to see&nbsp;
more questions logically answered.

